/**
 * teko::test — the surface a test uses to name things that belong to IT and to nobody else.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS (owner, 2026-07-30, reading the parallelism landed in this same change: "implementou-se
 * o paralelismo mas faltou o controle de concorrência, aí o arquivo acaba sofrendo sobrescrita"). The
 * first shared resource to be caught was the coverage dump, and it was fixed by ISOLATION — one file
 * per shard, then merged. That is the right answer and it was applied to exactly one resource. Every
 * other fixed scratch path a test writes has the same shape.
 *
 * AND THE SHARD SPLIT MAKES IT WORSE, by the same property that makes it a good split: it is
 * round-robin by ordinal, because per-test cost is uneven, so NEIGHBOURING tests land in DIFFERENT
 * processes — and neighbouring tests are precisely the ones sharing a scratch path, because they live
 * in the same file and the same family.
 *
 * SO THE DISCIPLINE IS UNIFORM, NOT PER-RESOURCE: a scratch path derives from the IDENTITY of the test
 * that writes it. Not a lock — isolation. A lock would serialise the very thing that was parallelised
 * and would still leave the last writer's bytes in the file for the next run to read.
 */

/**
 * scope_rt — the runtime's token for the test running right now.
 *
 * An FFI bottom because the identity lives where the per-test channel lives (src/runtime/teko_rt.c):
 * the channel already records the label, and the shard index is already parsed there.
 *
 * @return  `.s<shard>-<sanitised label>`, or "" when no test is running
 */
pub extern fn scope_rt() -> str = "tk_test_scope" from "teko_rt"

/**
 * scope — the token identifying the test running right now, or "" outside a test.
 *
 * EMPTY OUTSIDE A TEST IS THE POINT, not a fallback. The same path-composing code runs in PRODUCTION
 * (the regression runner asks the host toolchain the same questions the unit tests ask it), and there
 * the path must stay byte-for-byte what it was. Scoping applies exactly where the concurrency it
 * guards against exists.
 *
 * @return  a filesystem-safe token unique to the running test, or "" when none is running
 */
pub fn scope() -> str { scope_rt() }

/**
 * scoped — `base` rooted in THIS run and in the running test's own identity, so two tests can never
 * derive the same path even when they spell the same base, AND two runs can never derive the same path
 * even when two tests in them spell the same base.
 *
 * This is the ONE composer every scratch path in the corpus goes through, so "which paths are
 * isolated" is answerable by reading its call sites instead of by grepping for literals — which is how
 * the fixed paths got missed in the first place.
 *
 * DELEGATES TO `teko::journal::scratch`, which is the true composer (journaling-de-corrida-0.3.1 §2.2
 * / journal J2): `scratch` reads the SAME `teko::test::scope()` this function used to append raw, but
 * roots the result under this run's own `run_root()` instead of merely suffixing `base`. A caller that
 * only ever read this function's RETURN VALUE (never its exact shape) sees no change in behaviour: a
 * path unique to the running test, still ending in `base`, still "" outside a test.
 *
 * @param base  the path a test would otherwise have hard-coded
 * @return      `base` outside a test, a path unique to this run and this test inside one
 */
pub fn scoped(base: str) -> str { teko::journal::scratch(base) }

/** HOW_OK — the body returned normally. */
pub const HOW_OK: i32 = 0
/** HOW_PANICKED — the body panicked: an explicit `panic`, a failed assertion, or an implicit panic. */
pub const HOW_PANICKED: i32 = 1
/** HOW_EXITED — the body called `exit(n)`; `capture_probe_code` carries the n. */
pub const HOW_EXITED: i32 = 2

/** PROBE_RETURNS — the guard's body that returns normally. */
pub const PROBE_RETURNS: i32 = 0
/** PROBE_PANICS — the guard's body that panics explicitly. */
pub const PROBE_PANICS: i32 = 1
/** PROBE_EXITS — the guard's body that calls `exit(PROBE_EXIT_CODE)`. */
pub const PROBE_EXITS: i32 = 2
/** PROBE_DIV0 — the guard's body that divides by zero (the IMPLICIT panic). */
pub const PROBE_DIV0: i32 = 3
/** PROBE_EXIT_CODE — the value `PROBE_EXITS` tries to leave with, and which the capture hands back. */
pub const PROBE_EXIT_CODE: i32 = 7

/**
 * capture_probe — run one body of known ending under the runtime's capture and report how it ended.
 *
 * WHY THE BODIES LIVE IN THE RUNTIME AND NOT HERE, measured on this tree rather than assumed: the
 * design's surface for this is `run_capturing(body: cabi fn())`, and `cabi` is not a token this lexer
 * mints while `fn() -> T` in parameter position does not parse. Handing a Teko function's address to
 * an extern is a language feature that does not exist yet (crumb C1 of concorrencia-adiantada-s8).
 * The capture itself needs nothing from it — the `#test` harness is emitted C, where `&<symbol>` asks
 * the language for nothing — but the GUARD needs a way in, and a guard that cannot fail is
 * decoration. When `cabi fn` lands, this is replaced by `run_capturing` and the bodies move here.
 *
 * IT ANSWERS A SCALAR AND NOT A PAIR, and that too is measured: a `from "teko_rt"` extern cannot
 * return a user struct by value on this tree. The typer accepts a `Named` return for a teko_rt
 * extern, but codegen emits no prototype for one — it relies on `teko_rt.h` — and the header's C
 * struct type is not the mangled Teko one, so the generated call site is an `invalid initializer`.
 * The exit CODE is therefore a second read, `capture_probe_code`.
 *
 * @param which  one of the `PROBE_*` selectors
 * @return       one of the `HOW_*` values, or a value distinct from all of them for an unknown probe
 * @since 0.3.1
 */
pub extern fn capture_probe(which: i32) -> i32 = "tk_test_capture_probe" from "teko_rt"

/**
 * capture_probe_code — the exit value recorded by the most recent `capture_probe`.
 *
 * @return  the `exit(n)` value when the last probe ended by exiting; 0 otherwise
 * @since 0.3.1
 */
pub extern fn capture_probe_code() -> i32 = "tk_test_capture_last_code" from "teko_rt"
